#: src/macros/macro_rules/fragment-specifiers/exercise.md:6
msgid "Exercise: Pair Macro"
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/fragment-specifiers/exercise.md:8
msgid ""
"In this exercise, you will implement a macro named `pair!` that takes a "
"single syntactic element and doubles it into a tuple pair containing two of "
"that element: `(x, x)`."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/fragment-specifiers/exercise.md:12
msgid ""
"Your `pair!` macro should work across three different syntactic contexts:"
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/fragment-specifiers/exercise.md:14
msgid "**Types:** `pair!(i32)` expands to `(i32, i32)`."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/fragment-specifiers/exercise.md:15
msgid ""
"**Patterns:** `pair!(7)` and `pair!(_)` expand to `(7, 7)` and `(_, _)`, "
"respectively."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/fragment-specifiers/exercise.md:17
msgid "**Expressions:** `pair!(1 + 2)` expands to `(1 + 2, 1 + 2)`."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/fragment-specifiers/exercise.md:19
msgid "Instructions:"
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/fragment-specifiers/exercise.md:21
msgid ""
"Define the `pair!` macro with rules for types (`ty`), patterns (`pat`), and "
"expressions (`expr`)."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/fragment-specifiers/exercise.md:23
msgid ""
"Order the rules appropriately so that type names, patterns, and expressions "
"are correctly captured."
msgstr ""

